::ENG4_U03_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: Urban gardens can strengthen neighborhoods by increasing access to fresh food and creating shared public spaces. Volunteers distribute produce, host planting workshops, and maintain vacant lots that once attracted illegal dumping. What is the central idea? {
~A. Urban gardens only create illegal dumping.#This contradicts the passage.
=B. Urban gardens can support neighborhoods through food access and shared community spaces.#Correct. This captures both parts of the claim.
~C. Volunteers should avoid public spaces.#This is unsupported.
~D. Fresh food is unrelated to neighborhoods.#This contradicts the passage.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: Urban gardens can strengthen neighborhoods. Volunteers distribute produce, host planting workshops, and maintain vacant lots. Which support is most specific? {
~A. Urban gardens are good.#This is vague.
~B. Neighborhoods exist.#This is too general.
~C. Some people like gardens.#This is vague.
=D. Volunteers distribute produce and host planting workshops.#Correct. This names specific actions.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: The article states the central idea first, then groups support into food access and public-space benefits. Why is this organization effective? {
=A. It helps readers see two clear categories of evidence.#Correct. Grouping improves clarity.
~B. It makes the central idea harder to find.#The central idea appears first.
~C. It hides the support from readers.#The support is grouped.
~D. It removes the need for examples.#Examples are still needed.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Volunteers maintain vacant lots that once attracted illegal dumping. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Volunteers create more illegal dumping.#This reverses the meaning.
~B. Vacant lots cannot change.#This contradicts maintain.
=C. Volunteers care for formerly neglected lots.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~D. The passage is about private kitchens only.#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The support is relevant because it directly connects to the central idea. What does relevant mean? {
~A. Random#This is the opposite.
~B. Decorative#This is unrelated.
=C. Directly connected to the point#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~D. Hidden#This is not the meaning.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A paragraph claims that later school start times improve student well-being. It supports the claim by citing improved sleep duration but gives no information about grades, attendance, or mental health. Which evaluation is strongest? {
=A. The sleep evidence is relevant, but broader data would make the support more complete.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~B. The evidence is unrelated because sleep never affects well-being.#This is false.
~C. The support proves every possible effect.#The passage gives limited support.
~D. The claim has no support at all.#It has sleep evidence.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: Later school start times improve student well-being. Evidence: Students slept 37 minutes longer on average. What additional support would strengthen the central idea most? {
~A. A list of cafeteria menu items#This is unrelated.
~B. A story about school colors#This is unrelated.
~C. A definition of clocks only#This would not support well-being.
=D. Data about attendance, mood, or academic focus after the schedule change#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A text places sleep data before explaining the claim about well-being. What revision would improve structure? {
~A. Remove all data.#That would weaken support.
=B. State the central idea before presenting the data.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~C. Add unrelated sports scores.#This distracts from the claim.
~D. Put the conclusion before the topic and evidence.#This would confuse readers.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Students slept 37 minutes longer on average after the schedule changed. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Students slept less after the schedule changed.#This reverses the meaning.
~B. Every student slept exactly 37 minutes longer.#Average does not mean every student.
=C. Students gained an average of 37 minutes of sleep after the change.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~D. The schedule did not change.#The passage says it changed.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The data should be interpreted carefully because it is an average. What does interpreted mean here? {
=A. Explained or understood#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~B. Ignored#This is the opposite.
~C. Decorated#This is unrelated.
~D. Misspelled#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A text claims that public art benefits cities. Its only support is the sentence "Murals are colorful." What is the weakness? {
~A. The support is too specific and complete.#It is not complete.
~B. The claim has too much evidence.#It has too little.
~C. Public art cannot be discussed in informational text.#It can be discussed.
=D. The support is too limited to prove the broader claim.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A text claims that public art benefits cities. Which evidence would most strengthen the support? {
~A. A statement that blue is a color#This is too narrow.
=B. Survey results showing residents feel safer and more connected near maintained murals#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~C. A list of unrelated holidays#This is irrelevant.
~D. A sentence saying walls exist#This does not support the claim.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: An article about public art includes sections titled "Economic Effects," "Community Identity," and "Maintenance Costs." Why are these headings effective? {
~A. They hide the article's structure.#They reveal the structure.
=B. They show different dimensions of the topic and help readers compare effects.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~C. They remove the central idea.#They support the central idea.
~D. They replace evidence completely.#Headings do not replace evidence.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Public art may strengthen community identity when residents see local history represented in shared spaces. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Public art always erases local history.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Shared spaces cannot include art.#This is unsupported.
=C. Public art can support community identity by representing local history in places people share.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~D. Community identity is unrelated to representation.#This contradicts the passage.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The article evaluates several dimensions of public art. What does dimensions mean here? {
=A. Aspects or parts of an issue#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~B. Measurements only#This is too literal for the context.
~C. Typing errors#This is unrelated.
~D. Fictional characters#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A passage claims that online tutoring expands access. It supports the idea with one student's positive story but no information about cost, availability, or results for other students. Which evaluation is strongest? {
~A. The story proves the program works for everyone.#One story cannot prove that.
~B. The story is unrelated to tutoring.#It is about tutoring.
~C. No support could improve the passage.#Additional data could help.
=D. The personal story is relevant but insufficient by itself.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A passage claims that online tutoring expands access. Which support would best improve the passage? {
~A. A quote about favorite colors#This is unrelated.
~B. A list of keyboard brands#This is irrelevant.
=C. Data showing the number of students served, cost reductions, and availability by region#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~D. A sentence saying school exists#This is too general.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A passage uses one student's story before giving data about a tutoring program. Why might this structure be effective? {
=A. The story can create a human entry point before the data broadens the support.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~B. The story makes all data unnecessary.#Data is still useful.
~C. The structure hides the central idea.#It can introduce the idea.
~D. The structure turns the article into a poem.#It remains informational.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Online tutoring expanded access in rural areas where advanced courses were not always available. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Rural areas always had every advanced course.#This contradicts the passage.
=B. Online tutoring helped some rural students reach courses they might not otherwise have.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~C. Online tutoring reduced access.#This reverses the meaning.
~D. The passage is only about sports.#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The support is insufficient because it does not fully prove the central idea. What does insufficient mean? {
~A. More than enough#This is the opposite.
~B. Perfectly complete#This is the opposite.
~C. Decorative#This is unrelated.
=D. Not enough for the purpose#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A text claims that bike lanes improve commuting. It includes accident reduction data but no information about travel time or rider use. Which evaluation is best? {
=A. The safety data is relevant, but the support would be stronger with commuting data.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~B. Safety data is always irrelevant.#Safety can be relevant.
~C. The claim is fully proven by one type of data.#The claim includes commuting, which is broader.
~D. Bike lanes cannot be evaluated.#They can be evaluated.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A text claims that bike lanes improve commuting. Which additional support is most relevant? {
~A. The history of bicycle colors#This is not relevant.
~B. A recipe for cyclists#This is unrelated.
=C. Average commute times before and after bike-lane installation#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~D. A list of unrelated city festivals#This is irrelevant.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A bike-lane article groups evidence into "Safety," "Travel Time," and "Public Use." Why is this structure effective? {
~A. It removes evidence from the article.#The categories organize evidence.
~B. It makes the claim impossible to find.#The categories support the claim.
~C. It creates a fictional plot.#The article remains informational.
=D. It separates different types of support for easier evaluation.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Accident reduction data supports safety benefits but does not fully prove that commuting improved. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. The safety data proves every part of the claim.#This ignores the limitation.
=B. The safety data helps one part of the claim but does not prove the entire commuting claim.#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~C. The safety data is about cooking.#This is unrelated.
~D. Commuting is never affected by bike lanes.#This is unsupported.
}

::ENG4_U03_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The article should include more comprehensive support. What does comprehensive mean? {
=A. Covering the subject more completely#Correct. This answer matches the question and standard because it connects the evidence, reasoning, or rhetorical effect required by the task.
~B. Random and disconnected#This is the opposite.
~C. Decorative only#This is unrelated.
~D. Impossible to understand#This is not the meaning.
}
